{
  "configuration_id": "config_024",
  "reason": "Evidence: The current best is config_022 (multimodal, singleprofile, pca_n2000, imagenet_vit_l, concat) at 0.535 acc. RNA dominates over image (0.485 vs 0.208), and multimodal fusion adds ~5 points over RNA alone. The vit_l encoder slightly outperforms resnet50. However, all multimodal experiments so far use singleprofile; the impact of multiprofile with aggregation is completely untested and represents a major architectural decision. Question: Does switching from singleprofile to multiprofile with avgpool aggregation improve performance when combined with the best-known components (pca_n2000, imagenet_vit_l, concat)? Why this is the best next experiment: It extends the current best configuration with the simplest and most interpretable aggregation (avgpool), isolating the effect of multiprofile while holding all other variables at their best-known values. If multiprofile+avgpool helps, it opens the aggregation dimension (transformer) as a follow-up; if it hurts, we learn that singleprofile suffices and can redirect budget to fusion method or RNA encoder choices. This maximizes information gain per experiment under the limited budget."
}

